   Abuela had come up beside me. She read it twice, slowly, her lips
moving, the way she’d learned to read at night school thirty years ago,
for this. She put a palm on my cheek.
   “Ya,” she said. “Ya está.”
   That’s all she said.



I was standing alone at the edge of the floor when the room changed.
    It happened gently, like a lamp being turned up in a room where I’d
thought the lights were already on. The hall was still the hall, still lit,
still full. But the people had changed their clothes.
    The Armory, 2057.
    Brick scrubbed pale in some places and left smoky in others. The old
steel roof overhead. A banner above the coat table in plain letters:
COMMUNITY DANCE — JUNE 13, 2057. People turning on a floor the
color of honey. A woman in a wheelchair spinning a boy with one shoe
untied. At the edge of the floor, an old man resting between songs with
a cup in one hand and, behind his right ear, a very small device.
    I couldn’t see his face.
    I knew the way he held his knee.
    A child of about nine, with a lunch-box and no sense of occasion,
walked up to a drum. It had a visible repair around the rim. The old
man beside it moved his knee over to make room, the way you do
when you’re the one who brought the second copy and aren’t going to
be in the history and wouldn’t have it any other way.




